 "parts": [
  "A doctor can test a nerve in your hand with a bowl of warm water.",
  "Soak the fingers, then watch.",
  "Fingers with a damaged nerve don't wrinkle.",
  "Wait. Wrinkles come from your nerves?",
  "Yes. And that breaks the story most of us learned.",
  "The story says your skin soaks up water, like a sponge.",
  "Your nervous system tightens the tiny blood vessels in your fingertips.",
  "In one study, blood flow dropped right as the wrinkles appeared.",
  "But here's the bigger mystery. Why would your body do this on purpose?",
  "In twenty eleven, researchers suggested the wrinkles work like tire treads, channeling water away so you grip better.",
  "Two years later, volunteers moved wet marbles and weights. With wrinkled fingers, they were a little faster.",
  "Then a study of forty people found no change, in touch sensitivity or in handling wet objects.",
  "So do wrinkles help you grip? Scientists still debate it.",
  "Next time you're in the bath, check your fingertips.",
  "Those wrinkles aren't waterlogged skin. Your nerves drew them, and doctors can read them."
 ],
